%[text] # Plotting Spherical Energyscape Info
%[text] The `plotSphericalEnergyscape` function visualizes the directional content of 3D acoustic images from CLAIT beamforming algorithms. It reduces the range dimension of the image to one value per direction and plots these values over azimuth and elevation, on a Lambert azimuthal equal-area projection (default) or an equirectangular azimuth/elevation map. The plot includes a graticule, angle ticks, axis labels and a labelled colorbar.
%[text] This function is designed to work seamlessly with acoustic images generated by `clait.calculateAcousticImage()` for a set of directions that covers both azimuth and elevation.
%%
%[text] ## Namespace and Calling
%[text] The function is part of the CLAIT toolbox namespace and should be called using the full namespace qualifier:
clait.plotSphericalEnergyscape(imageData, structSensor, structImage, options)
%[text] ### Function Signatures
%[text] The function supports two calling modes:
%[text] #### Plotting Mode
[hPlot, hCbar] = clait.plotSphericalEnergyscape(imageData, structSensor, structImage, options)
%[text] #### Options Query Mode
opts = clait.plotSphericalEnergyscape('options')
%%
%[text] ## Input Parameters
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Parameter** | **Type** | **Required** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `imageData` | 2D double array | Yes | Acoustic image from CLAIT beamforming \[numRangeBins × numDirections\], or one value per direction |
%[text] | `structSensor` | struct | Yes | Sensor array configuration and signal characteristics |
%[text] | `structImage` | struct | Yes | Image formation parameters including the azimuth and elevation of each direction |
%[text] | `options` | struct | No | Visualization customization parameters (see detailed breakdown) |
%[text:table]
%[text]
%[text] ### **Detailed Breakdown of Input Structures**
%[text] #### 1. imageData
%[text] The 2D acoustic image matrix generated by `clait.calculateAcousticImage()`.
%[text] - **Dimensions**: `[numRangeBins × numDirections]`, one column per direction in `structImage`
%[text] - **Type**: Double array
%[text] - **Content**: Beamformed intensity values for each range and direction. Every column is reduced to one value (see `rangeReduction` and `rangeWindow`). A vector with one value per direction is plotted directly. \
%[text] #### 2. structSensor
%[text] Structure containing sensor array setup and signal characteristics, you can use the same one as used for `clait.calculateAcousticImage()`. It is only needed to convert `rangeWindow` from meters to range bins:
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Description** | **Units** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `sampleRate` | scalar | ADC sampling frequency | Hz |
%[text] | `numSamplesSensor` | scalar | Total number of samples in acquisition | samples |
%[text] | `speedOfSound` | scalar | Speed of sound in medium (default: 343) | m/s |
%[text:table]
%[text] ####
%[text] #### 3. structImage
%[text] Structure containing image formation parameters, you can use the same one as used for `clait.calculateAcousticImage()`:
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Description** | **Units** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `directionsAzimuth` | vector | Azimuth angle of each image column \[numDirections × 1\] | degrees |
%[text] | `directionsElevation` | vector | Elevation angle of each image column \[numDirections × 1\] | degrees |
%[text] | `decimationFactor` | scalar | Decimation factor applied to acoustic image, used for `rangeWindow` (default: 10, as `calculateAcousticImage`) | dimensionless |
%[text:table]
%[text] The directions do not have to lie on a regular grid. They are triangulated after projection, so any set of directions that covers an area of the sphere can be plotted. `clait.generateDirections` makes regular grids as well as nearly uniform patterns (`'equalarea'`, `'fibonacci'`, `'hexagonal'`), for example a 3 degree grid over the front hemisphere:
[structImage.directionsAzimuth, structImage.directionsElevation] = clait.generateDirections('grid', struct('spacing', 3));
%[text] ####
%[text] #### 4. options (Optional)
%[text] Structure containing visualization parameters. Use `clait.plotSphericalEnergyscape('options')` to retrieve all available options with defaults.
%[text] **Projection Options:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Default** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `projection` | string | `'lambert'` | `'lambert'` = Lambert azimuthal equal-area projection centred on the sensor axis, the front hemisphere becomes a disk. `'equirectangular'` = azimuth and elevation directly on the axes |
%[text] | `azimuthPositiveLeft` | logical | `true` | Draw positive azimuth on the left, as seen from the sensor (same as `plotPolarEnergyscape`) |
%[text:table]
%[text]
%[text] **Range Reduction Options:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Default** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `rangeReduction` | string | `'max'` | `'max'` = peak amplitude over range, `'energy'` = sum of squares over range |
%[text] | `rangeWindow` | 1×2 vector | `[]` | `[minRange maxRange]` in m to only use part of the range, `[]` = all ranges |
%[text:table]
%[text]
%[text] **Scale Options:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Default** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `useDbScale` | logical | `true` | `true` = dB scale, `false` = linear scale. `'max'` uses 20·log10 (amplitude), `'energy'` uses 10·log10 (power) |
%[text] | `dbCut` | scalar | `-60` | Lower dB limit relative to peak (dB) |
%[text] | `dbMax` | scalar | `0` | Upper dB limit relative to peak (dB) |
%[text] | `linearMin` | scalar | `0` | Minimum value for linear scale normalization |
%[text] | `linearMax` | scalar | `1` | Maximum value for linear scale normalization |
%[text] | `normalizeToMax` | logical | `true` | Normalize data to its maximum value |
%[text] | `colormap` | N×3 array | `parula(1024)` | Colormap matrix |
%[text] | `backgroundColor` | color spec | `'w'` | Background color |
%[text:table]
%[text]
%[text] **Grid Display Options:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Default** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `showGrid` | logical | `true` | Display the graticule (meridians and parallels) |
%[text] | `gridAzimuthSpacing` | scalar | `30` | Spacing between meridians (degrees) |
%[text] | `gridElevationSpacing` | scalar | `30` | Spacing between parallels (degrees) |
%[text] | `gridColor` | RGB or string | `'k'` | Grid line color |
%[text] | `gridLineWidth` | scalar | `0.75` | Grid line width |
%[text:table]
%[text]
%[text] **Display and Labeling Options:**
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Field** | **Type** | **Default** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `showLabels` | logical | `true` | Display angle labels |
%[text] | `labelStyle` | string | `'axes'` | `'axes'` = angle ticks and axis labels on the axes: azimuth ticks where the meridians cross the equator, elevation ticks where the parallels cross the central meridian. `'graticule'` = angle labels on the map next to the grid lines (azimuth along the equator, elevation along the left edge), axes hidden |
%[text] | `labelFontSize` | scalar | `9` | Font size of the `'graticule'` labels |
%[text] | `labelColor` | color spec | `'k'` | Color of the `'graticule'` labels |
%[text] | `xlabel` | string | `'Azimuth (°)'` | X-axis label (`'axes'` style) |
%[text] | `ylabel` | string | `'Elevation (°)'` | Y-axis label (`'axes'` style) |
%[text] | `title` | string | `''` | Plot title |
%[text] | `showColorbar` | logical | `true` | Display colorbar |
%[text] | `colorbarLabel` | string | `''` | Colorbar label, `''` = automatic from the scale |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Output
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Output** | **Type** | **Description** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `hPlot` | graphics handle | Handle to the patch object of the image |
%[text] | `hCbar` | graphics handle | Handle to the colorbar object, or `[]` if colorbar is not shown |
%[text:table]
%[text] To share one colorbar between several plots in a `tiledlayout`, show the colorbar only on the last plot and move it with `hCbar.Layout.Tile = 'east'`. The example below shows this.
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%[text] A full example is available, including a recreation of the point spread function figure of the publication. To quickly open it after installing the toolbox, run the following command:
clait.openClaitPlottingSphericalEnergyscapeExample

